---
import '../styles/global.css';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Navbar from '../components/Navbar.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

// Post-event mode (event was 2026-07-03). When true, hides all
// nomination content but keeps confirmed honoree cards visible.
// Preserved in code so the entire nomination structure can be
// revived for the next event by flipping this to false. Added
// 2026-07-10 per Mike.
const POST_EVENT_MODE = true;

const pastCategories = [
  {
    icon: "🎖️",
    title: "Military & Veterans",
    desc: "Those who have served our nation in uniform with extraordinary distinction"
  },
  {
    icon: "🚒",
    title: "First Responders",
    desc: "Police officers, firefighters, paramedics, and others who protect our communities every day"
  },
  {
    icon: "🏥",
    title: "Healthcare & Medicine",
    desc: "Medical professionals whose innovations and compassion have saved countless lives"
  },
  {
    icon: "📚",
    title: "Education & Mentorship",
    desc: "Teachers, coaches, and mentors who shape the next generation of American leaders"
  },
  {
    icon: "🤝",
    title: "Community Service & Philanthropy",
    desc: "Citizens whose generosity and volunteer efforts have strengthened communities across America"
  },
  {
    icon: "🇺🇸",
    title: "Public Service & Leadership",
    desc: "Leaders in government and civic life who have dedicated their careers to the public good"
  },
];

// Honoree feature cards (mirrors /icons/). Added 2026-05-21 per Mike.
// img: null renders a name placeholder box until a headshot is provided.
//
// 2026-06-01: Added Luis Avila, Claudia Avila, Deborah Snyder, and
// Maria Grinstead. Luis and Deborah bios + photos pulled from the
// 2025 inaugural ball backup (allamericanball.com archive). Claudia
// and Maria need Mike to provide bio details + photos.
const heroHonorees = [
  {
    name: "Elizabeth Dole",
    img: "/images/event/elizabeth-dole.jpg",
    role: "U.S. Senator · Cabinet Secretary · Humanitarian",
    bio: "Elizabeth Dole has devoted her life to service at the highest levels of American leadership &mdash; one of the few people in history to serve in the Cabinets of two presidents, as Secretary of Transportation under Ronald Reagan and Secretary of Labor under George H. W. Bush.",
    bioExpanded: "<p>She later served as President and CEO of the American Red Cross, leading one of the nation&rsquo;s most important humanitarian institutions through a period of modernization and expanded global impact, before being elected to the United States Senate, representing North Carolina with distinction.</p><p>In the years since, her legacy has only deepened through the Elizabeth Dole Foundation. What began as a personal mission has grown into a nationwide movement &mdash; raising hundreds of millions of dollars, driving landmark policy and legislative advances, and fundamentally reshaping how the nation recognizes and supports military and veteran caregivers. Millions of caregivers who were once invisible are now seen, supported, and woven into the national conversation.</p><p>Senator Dole exemplifies an All-American Hero because her life reflects the very best of what this country asks of its citizens: a deep sense of duty, an unwavering commitment to others, and the courage to lead when it matters most. An All-American Hero leaves the country stronger than they found it &mdash; and Senator Elizabeth Dole has done exactly that, with grace, integrity, and a lifetime of impact.</p>",
  },
  {
    name: "Captain Luis Avila",
    img: "/images/event/luis-avila.jpg",
    role: "Captain, U.S. Army · Combat-Wounded Warrior · Bronze Star &amp; Purple Heart Recipient",
    bio: "Captain Luis Avila joined the United States Army in 2000 and served as a Military Police Officer through five combat deployments &mdash; including Operation Noble Eagle, three tours in Operation Iraqi Freedom, and Operation Enduring Freedom in Afghanistan, where a mounted IED explosion on December 27, 2011 ended his combat career and began a remarkable journey of recovery, service, and advocacy.",
    bioExpanded: "<p>Born and raised with a deep sense of duty, Luis enlisted in 2000 and was commissioned in 2004. He earned a bachelor&rsquo;s degree in Accounting and Computer Science and a master&rsquo;s in Business and Organizational Security Management from Webster University. As a Military Police Officer he served in a series of demanding leadership roles &mdash; Force Protection/Safety Officer, Company Executive Officer, Assistant Operations Officer, Assistant S3, and Company Commander &mdash; before leading his company from the 720th Military Police Battalion in Afghanistan.</p><p>The IED that struck his convoy in December 2011 took his left leg and caused two heart attacks, two strokes, and an anoxic and traumatic brain injury that left him almost completely paralyzed. He was pronounced dead three times. He arrived back in the United States in a coma and on life support, awoke unable to see or eat, and could not speak for more than three years. Through music, speech, physical, and occupational therapy &mdash; along with adaptive technology and his own extraordinary perseverance &mdash; he continues to regain his abilities every day.</p><p>Today Luis is one of the country&rsquo;s most powerful voices for wounded warriors. He has performed more than 100 times in support of the military &mdash; at the National Intrepid Center of Excellence at Walter Reed, the Supreme Court, the U.S. Capitol, and at galas and memorials across the country. He shared a nationally broadcast performance of &ldquo;God Bless America&rdquo; alongside Ren&eacute;e Fleming and the National Symphony Orchestra at the 2017 Memorial Day Capital Concert, and regularly performs with the Army, Air Force, and Marine Corps bands.</p><p>An adaptive athlete of remarkable grit, Luis has completed five Army Ten-Milers on his recumbent bicycle and earned First Place Amputee Warrior Men. He was a member of Team Army at the 2024 DOD Warrior Games, taking home five gold medals and one bronze, and joined Team USA at the 2025 Invictus Games. His honors include the Bronze Star, the Purple Heart, and The Order of the Marechaussee in Silver. He lives in Maryland with his family and continues his rehabilitation at Walter Reed National Military Medical Center in Bethesda.</p>",
  },
  {
    name: "Claudia Avila",
    img: "/images/event/claudia-avila.jpg",
    role: "Military Caregiver · Wife of Captain Luis Avila",
    bio: "Claudia Avila has spent more than a decade as the steadfast caregiver and advocate alongside her husband, Captain Luis Avila, following the IED explosion that nearly took his life in Afghanistan in December 2011.",
  },
  {
    name: "LTC (R) Deborah Snyder",
    img: "/images/event/deborah-snyder.jpg",
    role: "LTC (R), U.S. Army · Founder &amp; CEO, Operation Renewed Hope Foundation",
    bio: "Deborah Snyder is an Army veteran, decorated combat helicopter pilot, and the founder of Operation Renewed Hope Foundation &mdash; the Alexandria, Virginia-based non-profit she built to help end homelessness among America&rsquo;s veterans, which has directly assisted more than 1,500 veterans and their family members.",
    bioExpanded: "<p>A native of Alexandria, Virginia, Deborah retired in 2009 as a Lieutenant Colonel after 22 years of service in the U.S. Army Aviation Branch. A Magna cum laude graduate of James Madison University and a distinguished military graduate of JMU&rsquo;s Reserve Officer Training Corps program, she holds a Bachelor of Science in Political Science and Criminal Justice and a Master&rsquo;s in Military Studies.</p><p>Across her career she served in a wide range of leadership positions &mdash; Platoon and Company Commander, Operations Officer, Branch Chief, Liaison Officer, and Division Chief &mdash; with extensive experience managing programs at both the tactical and strategic levels. Her formal military training includes the Aviation Advanced and Basic Courses, Air Assault and Airborne Schools, UH-1 &ldquo;Huey&rdquo; and UH-60 Blackhawk Flight Training, the Test Pilot Course, the Combined Arms and Service Staff School, Marine Command and Staff College, and the Army Force Management School. She is a decorated combat veteran.</p><p>After her military retirement, Deborah served the Department of the Army for an additional ten years as a Civilian Analyst before turning her full attention to those who had served alongside her. In 2011 she founded Operation Renewed Hope Foundation specifically to help homeless veterans, and now leads a team of professionals equally dedicated to the mission. Under her leadership the foundation has helped more than 1,500 veterans and family members move out of homelessness and into stable lives &mdash; a remarkable record of impact built one veteran at a time.</p>",
  },
  // 2026-06-17: Added Captain Eugene B. "Red" McDaniel — Vietnam POW,
  // Navy Cross recipient, founder of the American Defense Institute.
  // Bio sourced from bio(RedMcDaniel)2021.pdf in Mike's Downloads.
  // Card portrait = modern headshot (IMG_3653). bioExpanded also
  // includes the iconic 1973 Hanoi release photo (IMG_3604) and a
  // post-release Navy command photo (IMG_2475) inline — Red's arc
  // (POW → carrier CO → defense advocate) reads stronger with visuals.
  {
    name: "Captain Eugene B. “Red” McDaniel",
    img: "/images/event/red-mcdaniel.jpg",
    role: "Captain, U.S. Navy (Retired) · Vietnam POW · Navy Cross Recipient",
    bio: "On May 19, 1967, on his 81st combat mission over North Vietnam, Captain Eugene B. &ldquo;Red&rdquo; McDaniel was shot down while flying his A-6 Intruder. He survived six years as a Prisoner of War &mdash; among the most brutally tortured American POWs of the Vietnam War &mdash; before his release on March 4, 1973. He returned home with the Navy Cross, two Silver Stars, and two Purple Hearts, and went on to command an aircraft carrier and found a leading national defense non-profit.",
    bioExpanded: "<p>Listed as &ldquo;missing in action&rdquo; until 1970, when the Hanoi government finally acknowledged him as a prisoner, Captain McDaniel endured six years in a communist prison. The torture he suffered stemmed directly from his active role in camp communications during an organized escape attempt by his fellow prisoners &mdash; leadership that came at extraordinary personal cost. His account of those years, <em>Scars and Stripes</em>, has become a defining record of American resilience under captivity.</p><figure class='my-6'><img src='/images/event/red-mcdaniel-hanoi-release-1973.jpg' alt='Commander Eugene Red McDaniel upon his release in Hanoi, March 4, 1973, after six years as a POW' class='w-full rounded-lg shadow-md' loading='lazy' decoding='async' /><figcaption class='text-center text-xs text-slate/60 mt-2 italic'>CDR Eugene &ldquo;Red&rdquo; McDaniel upon his release in Hanoi, March 4, 1973 &mdash; after six years as a prisoner of war.</figcaption></figure><p>When he returned home from Vietnam, Captain McDaniel was awarded the Navy&rsquo;s highest decoration for bravery, the Navy Cross. Among his other military honors are two Silver Stars, the Legion of Merit with Combat &ldquo;V&rdquo;, the Distinguished Flying Cross, three Bronze Stars with Combat &ldquo;V&rdquo;, and two Purple Hearts for wounds received at the hands of his North Vietnamese captors.</p><p>After his release, Captain McDaniel resumed active duty, serving as Commanding Officer of USS NIAGARA FALLS and Commanding Officer of the aircraft carrier USS LEXINGTON. He later served as Director of Navy/Marine Corps Liaison to the U.S. House of Representatives before retiring from the Navy in 1982.</p><figure class='my-6'><img src='/images/event/red-mcdaniel-navy-captain.jpg' alt='Captain Red McDaniel in Navy dress whites with full medals during his post-release Navy command' class='w-full rounded-lg shadow-md' loading='lazy' decoding='async' /><figcaption class='text-center text-xs text-slate/60 mt-2 italic'>Captain McDaniel during his post-release Navy command, his chest bearing the Navy Cross, Silver Stars, Distinguished Flying Cross, and Purple Hearts earned in service to his country.</figcaption></figure><p>Following retirement, Captain McDaniel founded the American Defense Institute &mdash; a non-profit organization dedicated to increasing public awareness of the need for a strong national defense. Captain McDaniel and his wife Dorothy reside in Alexandria, Virginia. They have three grown children, nine grandchildren, and four great-grandchildren.</p>",
  },
  // 2026-06-17: Added The Honorable Rob Wilkins — retired USAF Master
  // Sergeant, nationally recognized military fitness advocate, Senior
  // Advisor for Veteran Fitness and Wellness at The Platform Magazine,
  // and former member of the President's Council on Sports, Fitness &
  // Nutrition under both President Trump and President Biden. Bio
  // sourced from The Platform Magazine. Portrait taken with the U.S.
  // Capitol in the background.
  {
    name: "The Honorable Rob Wilkins",
    img: "/images/event/rob-wilkins.jpg",
    role: "Retired U.S. Air Force Master Sergeant · Senior Advisor for Veteran Fitness &amp; Wellness, The Platform Magazine",
    bio: "The Honorable Rob Wilkins is a retired United States Air Force Master Sergeant and nationally recognized military fitness advocate, serving as Senior Advisor for Veteran Fitness and Wellness at The Platform Magazine. A former member of the President&rsquo;s Council on Sports, Fitness &amp; Nutrition under both President Trump and President Biden, he is widely respected for his leadership in military wellness, human performance, veteran advocacy, and national fitness initiatives supporting service members, veterans, and military families.",
    bioExpanded: "<p>Master Sergeant Wilkins served in the United States Air Force before retiring to dedicate his life to advancing the physical and mental wellness of service members, veterans, and military families. His work bridges military readiness, human performance science, and veteran advocacy at the highest levels of national policy.</p><p>His appointment to the President&rsquo;s Council on Sports, Fitness &amp; Nutrition under two consecutive administrations &mdash; President Trump and President Biden &mdash; reflects rare bipartisan recognition of his expertise and impact. The President&rsquo;s Council shapes national fitness initiatives reaching tens of millions of Americans, and Wilkins has used his platform to ensure that military and veteran wellness remain at the center of the conversation.</p><p>As Senior Advisor for Veteran Fitness and Wellness at <em>The Platform Magazine</em>, Wilkins continues to drive programs, partnerships, and public awareness that support service members, veterans, and their families &mdash; embodying the spirit of an All-American Hero through a lifetime of service that did not end at retirement.</p><p class='mt-4'><a href='https://fitness.foundation/wilkins' target='_blank' rel='noopener noreferrer' class='text-gold hover:text-gold-light underline font-semibold'>Learn more about Rob Wilkins&rsquo; work &nearr;</a></p>",
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="All-American Heroes Award | 2026 Honorees | Countdown 250 Ball Washington DC" description="Meet the All-American Heroes recognized at the historic Countdown 250 Ball on July 3, 2026 at the Washington Hilton in Washington, D.C. — modern-day American heroes celebrated for their lifetime of extraordinary service." ogImage="/images/event/buzz-aldrin.png">
  <Navbar />
  <main
    class="relative"
    style="background: radial-gradient(circle at 30% 30%, rgba(4, 61, 156, 0.45) 0%, transparent 55%), linear-gradient(135deg, #052058 0%, #002B77 50%, #043D9C 100%); background-attachment: fixed;"
  >
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- HERO — canonical sub-page pattern (same as hosted-group-tables  -->
    <!-- and entertainment-preview). 16:9 image, full-bleed, gold title -->
    <!-- ribbon flush-left. -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section class="relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="relative w-full h-[55vh] sm:h-[60vh] min-h-[320px] max-h-[680px]">
        <img
          src="/images/event/header-honorees-flag-medal.jpg"
          alt="The All-American Heroes Lifetime Achievement Award medal — a gold medallion with red, white, and blue ribbon — resting at center on the rippling stars and stripes of the American flag"
          class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full object-cover"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <div class="absolute inset-x-0 top-0 h-32 bg-gradient-to-b from-navy-dark/55 to-transparent pointer-events-none"></div>

        <div class="absolute inset-y-0 left-0 flex items-end pb-4 sm:pb-8 md:pb-12 lg:pb-16 pointer-events-none">
          <div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-gold to-[#f4d847] py-2.5 sm:py-3.5 md:py-4 lg:py-5 pl-3 sm:pl-8 md:pl-14 lg:pl-20 xl:pl-28 pr-5 sm:pr-8 md:pr-12 lg:pr-14 border-y-2 border-r-2 border-navy-dark/35 rounded-r-2xl shadow-[0_14px_32px_-10px_rgba(0,0,0,0.55)] pointer-events-auto">
            <h1 class="text-xl sm:text-3xl md:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-bold text-navy font-heading leading-none tracking-tight">
              All-American Heroes
            </h1>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- About the Awards -->
    <section class="py-20 ">
      <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6">
        <!-- Eyebrow (moved here from the hero overlay 2026-05-06 when
             the hero adopted the image-led pattern). -->
        <p class="text-white font-bold tracking-[0.3em] uppercase text-xs sm:text-sm text-center mb-8">
          Honoring Those Who Serve
        </p>

        <div class="text-center mb-12">
          <!-- All-American Heroes medal — anchor the section visually -->
          <picture>
            <source srcset="/images/misc/all-amer-heroes-medal.webp" type="image/webp" />
            <img
              src="/images/misc/all-amer-heroes-medal.png"
              alt="All-American Heroes Lifetime Achievement Award medal"
              class="w-32 sm:w-40 md:w-48 h-auto mx-auto mb-6"
              style="filter: drop-shadow(0 14px 28px rgba(0,0,0,0.18)) drop-shadow(0 4px 10px rgba(197,164,78,0.32));"
              loading="eager"
            />
          </picture>
          <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-gold font-heading leading-tight mb-2">All-American Heroes</h2>
          <p class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gold font-heading leading-tight mb-5">Lifetime Achievement Awards</p>
          <div class="w-16 h-1 bg-gold mx-auto mb-6"></div>
          <p class="text-white/75 text-lg max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
            Recognizing extraordinary Americans whose lifetime of service, sacrifice, and leadership embodies the very best of our nation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="text-white/70 text-lg leading-relaxed space-y-6">
          <p>
            Every four years, in conjunction with the Presidential Inauguration, the All-American Ball presents the
            <strong class="text-gold">All-American Heroes Awards</strong> to a select group of individuals who have
            dedicated their lives to serving others. These awards honor Americans whose courage, commitment, and
            selflessness have made a lasting impact on our nation.
          </p>
          <p>
            At the <strong class="text-gold">Official Countdown 250 Ball</strong> on July 3, 2026, we continue
            this proud tradition — presenting Lifetime Achievement Awards to outstanding citizens who represent
            the very best of America.
          </p>
          <p>
            Past honorees have included decorated military heroes, selfless first responders, pioneering astronauts,
            groundbreaking educators, tireless community leaders, and other extraordinary Americans whose stories
            inspire us all. Each honoree is recognized on stage during a stirring awards ceremony that promises to
            be one of the most powerful and memorable moments of your life.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- HONOREES — feature cards (mirrors /icons/). Added 2026-05-21  -->
    <!-- per Mike. Photo-less honorees show a name placeholder until a -->
    <!-- headshot is provided. "More announcements" teaser sits at top. -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
      <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6">
        <div class="text-center mb-8 sm:mb-10">
          <p class="text-gold font-bold tracking-[0.3em] uppercase text-xs sm:text-sm mb-3">Honorees</p>
          <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold text-gold font-heading leading-tight">Celebrating American Heroes</h2>
          <div class="w-16 h-1 bg-gold mx-auto mt-5 mb-6"></div>
          <p class="text-gold font-bold tracking-[0.25em] uppercase text-sm sm:text-base">More Major Announcements Coming Soon</p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-8">
          {heroHonorees.map((h) => (
            <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-navy-dark via-navy to-navy-dark border border-gold/40 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-[0_18px_40px_-16px_rgba(0,0,0,0.6)] flex flex-col sm:flex-row">
              <div class="sm:w-2/5 sm:flex-shrink-0 bg-navy/50">
                <div class="aspect-[4/3] sm:aspect-auto sm:h-full overflow-hidden">
                  {h.img ? (
                    <img src={h.img} alt={h.name} class="w-full h-full object-cover" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
                  ) : (
                    <div class="w-full h-full min-h-[220px] flex items-center justify-center bg-gradient-to-br from-navy via-navy-dark to-navy p-6 text-center">
                      <div>
                        <picture>
                          <source srcset="/images/misc/all-amer-heroes-medal.webp" type="image/webp" />
                          <img
                            src="/images/misc/all-amer-heroes-medal.png"
                            alt={`All-American Heroes Lifetime Achievement Award medal — placeholder for ${h.name}`}
                            class="w-32 sm:w-36 md:w-40 h-auto mx-auto"
                            style="filter: drop-shadow(0 12px 24px rgba(0,0,0,0.32)) drop-shadow(0 4px 10px rgba(197,164,78,0.38));"
                            loading="lazy"
                            decoding="async"
                          />
                        </picture>
                        <p class="text-white/45 text-[10px] sm:text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] mt-5">Photo Coming Soon</p>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  )}
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="flex-1 p-6 sm:p-8 md:p-10">
                <h3 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-gold font-heading leading-tight mb-1">{h.name}</h3>
                <p class="text-white/55 text-xs sm:text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.18em] font-semibold mb-5">{h.role}</p>
                <details class="text-white/75 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed group">
                  <summary class="list-none cursor-pointer marker:hidden">
                    <span class="block" set:html={h.bio} />
                    <span class="mt-3 inline-block text-gold hover:text-gold-light font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-xs group-open:hidden">Read more &rarr;</span>
                  </summary>
                  <div class="mt-4 space-y-4" set:html={h.bioExpanded} />
                  <span class="mt-3 hidden group-open:inline-block text-gold hover:text-gold-light font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-xs cursor-pointer" onclick="this.closest('details').open = false">&larr; Show less</span>
                </details>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Past Categories -->
    <section class="py-12 sm:py-14 ">
      <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6">
        <div class="text-center mb-7">
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-gold mb-3 font-heading">Fields of Service</h2>
          <p class="text-white/65 text-base sm:text-lg max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-snug">All-American Heroes Award recipients can come from any background or career, but all share a common thread &mdash; a lifetime of extraordinary service to others.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4 sm:gap-5">
          {pastCategories.map((cat) => (
            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-4 sm:p-5 shadow-sm border border-slate/5">
              <div class="text-2xl mb-2">{cat.icon}</div>
              <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy mb-1.5">{cat.title}</h3>
              <p class="text-slate/65 text-sm leading-snug">{cat.desc}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- What Makes an All-American Hero -->
    <section class="py-12 sm:py-14 ">
      <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6">
        <div class="text-center mb-6">
          <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold text-gold mb-3 font-heading">What Makes an All-American Hero?</h2>
          <div class="w-16 h-1 bg-gold mx-auto"></div>
        </div>

        <p class="text-center text-white/70 text-base sm:text-lg leading-snug mb-6">Award recipients are chosen based on the following criteria:</p>

        <!-- Inline list — each criterion is title + em-dash + description on
             one line (or two on narrow screens) for a much tighter footprint. -->
        <div class="space-y-3 sm:space-y-4">
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3 sm:gap-4">
            <svg class="w-7 h-7 flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5 drop-shadow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true">
              <defs>
                <linearGradient id="liquidBlue1" x1="0%" y1="0%" x2="0%" y2="100%">
                  <stop offset="0%" style="stop-color:#6ea0e8"/>
                  <stop offset="50%" style="stop-color:#1a4ba8"/>
                  <stop offset="100%" style="stop-color:#001a45"/>
                </linearGradient>
              </defs>
              <path fill="url(#liquidBlue1)" stroke="#C5A44E" stroke-width="0.6" d="M12 2 L14.39 8.45 L21.11 8.45 L15.86 12.55 L18.25 19 L12 14.91 L5.75 19 L8.14 12.55 L2.89 8.45 L9.61 8.45 Z"/>
            </svg>
            <p class="text-white/80 text-sm sm:text-base leading-snug">
              <strong class="text-gold font-bold">Lifetime of Service</strong> &mdash; A sustained, long-term commitment to serving others; not a single act, but a lifetime of dedication.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3 sm:gap-4">
            <svg class="w-7 h-7 flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5 drop-shadow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true">
              <defs>
                <linearGradient id="liquidBlue2" x1="0%" y1="0%" x2="0%" y2="100%">
                  <stop offset="0%" style="stop-color:#6ea0e8"/>
                  <stop offset="50%" style="stop-color:#1a4ba8"/>
                  <stop offset="100%" style="stop-color:#001a45"/>
                </linearGradient>
              </defs>
              <path fill="url(#liquidBlue2)" stroke="#C5A44E" stroke-width="0.6" d="M12 2 L14.39 8.45 L21.11 8.45 L15.86 12.55 L18.25 19 L12 14.91 L5.75 19 L8.14 12.55 L2.89 8.45 L9.61 8.45 Z"/>
            </svg>
            <p class="text-white/80 text-sm sm:text-base leading-snug">
              <strong class="text-gold font-bold">Selflessness &amp; Sacrifice</strong> &mdash; A demonstrated pattern of putting others before oneself, often at personal cost or risk.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3 sm:gap-4">
            <svg class="w-7 h-7 flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5 drop-shadow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true">
              <defs>
                <linearGradient id="liquidBlue3" x1="0%" y1="0%" x2="0%" y2="100%">
                  <stop offset="0%" style="stop-color:#6ea0e8"/>
                  <stop offset="50%" style="stop-color:#1a4ba8"/>
                  <stop offset="100%" style="stop-color:#001a45"/>
                </linearGradient>
              </defs>
              <path fill="url(#liquidBlue3)" stroke="#C5A44E" stroke-width="0.6" d="M12 2 L14.39 8.45 L21.11 8.45 L15.86 12.55 L18.25 19 L12 14.91 L5.75 19 L8.14 12.55 L2.89 8.45 L9.61 8.45 Z"/>
            </svg>
            <p class="text-white/80 text-sm sm:text-base leading-snug">
